WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.700
A mouse licks sugar water and keeps licking.

2
00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:08.100
What has its brain just worked out?

3
00:00:09.050 --> 00:00:10.810
Two questions hide in that licking.

4
00:00:11.690 --> 00:00:13.190
Which taste does its brain register?

5
00:00:14.110 --> 00:00:15.710
And how much does the mouse want it?

6
00:00:16.583 --> 00:00:18.303
Are those one signal or two?

7
00:00:19.123 --> 00:00:20.503
And where can they be pulled apart?

8
00:00:21.517 --> 00:00:24.217
Call the first one identity: which taste it is.

9
00:00:25.117 --> 00:00:30.077
Call the second one value: whether the animal is drawn to it or turns away, and how

10
00:00:30.077 --> 00:00:33.377
strongly. Its good-or-bad sign is called valence.

11
00:00:34.317 --> 00:00:35.817
Taste cells are not neurons.

12
00:00:36.757 --> 00:00:38.317
They must hand their news to a fibre.

13
00:00:39.417 --> 00:00:44.697
In mice, type II cells include populations that respond mainly to sweet, to bitter

14
00:00:45.037 --> 00:00:48.377
or to umami. Some cells respond to more than one.

15
00:00:49.350 --> 00:00:51.010
Type III cells sense acid.

16
00:00:51.850 --> 00:00:54.170
They make conventional synapses onto the fibre.

17
00:00:55.250 --> 00:00:58.610
Type II cells have no conventional vesicular synapse.

18
00:00:59.370 --> 00:01:01.110
They release ATP through a channel.

19
00:01:02.133 --> 00:01:06.313
On its own, the main channel subunit opens slowly in cultured cells.

20
00:01:07.233 --> 00:01:09.333
Its time constant is over half a second.

21
00:01:10.383 --> 00:01:12.243
A type II cell's spike is brief.

22
00:01:13.163 --> 00:01:14.403
Can a gate that slow keep up?

23
00:01:18.317 --> 00:01:23.657
Barely. Adding a partner subunit speeds opening to about ten milliseconds.

24
00:01:24.797 --> 00:01:27.877
These are cultured-cell times, not delays in a living mouse.

25
00:01:28.900 --> 00:01:34.420
in mice lacking that partner, taste buds released no ATP in response to tastes.

26
00:01:35.433 --> 00:01:39.433
Fibres from the front of the tongue have their cell bodies in the geniculate

27
00:01:39.433 --> 00:01:40.433
ganglion.

28
00:01:40.933 --> 00:01:45.813
In rodents, signals then climb through brainstem relays and the thalamus to taste

29
00:01:45.813 --> 00:01:47.873
cortex, and on to the amygdala.

30
00:01:49.017 --> 00:01:53.817
The experiments here sample three stations: the ganglion, the cortex and the

31
00:01:53.817 --> 00:01:54.817
amygdala.

32
00:01:55.267 --> 00:01:56.647
Here is the one idea.

33
00:01:56.647 --> 00:02:01.447
In the mouse, identity begins with which taste-cell population is driven.

34
00:02:02.467 --> 00:02:07.167
Many fibres pass that on selectively at low strength, and more broadly at high

35
00:02:07.167 --> 00:02:09.327
strength. Not all of them broaden.

36
00:02:10.400 --> 00:02:14.240
Drive the sweet field of taste cortex, and a mouse is drawn in.

37
00:02:15.020 --> 00:02:18.080
Block that field, and trained mice struggle to recognize sweet.

38
00:02:19.133 --> 00:02:21.393
Drive the bitter field, and it turns away.

39
00:02:22.383 --> 00:02:25.343
Then one manipulation pulled those two readouts apart.

40
00:02:26.317 --> 00:02:29.317
Silence the amygdala, and mice still recognize sweet.

41
00:02:30.117 --> 00:02:32.997
But driving sweet cortex no longer draws them in.

42
00:02:33.950 --> 00:02:36.330
And the pull changes with what the body needs.

43
00:02:37.350 --> 00:02:39.170
The experiments here are in mice.

44
00:02:40.190 --> 00:02:41.490
None of this is a human map.

45
00:02:42.400 --> 00:02:47.580
Which taste, and how good: one cortical field bears on both, and silencing the

46
00:02:47.580 --> 00:02:50.020
amygdala separates them in these mouse assays.
